module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_rollback_depends_on_a_credential_that_can_expire_mid_actuation

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data a_rollback_depends_on_a_credential_that_can_expire_mid_actuation: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_rollback_depends_on_a_credential_that_can_expire_mid_actuation" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a rollback depends on a credential that can expire mid-actuation** (an out-of-band actuation mutates a live resource, and its restore step must RE-FETCH a credential to undo the mutation. The credential has a lifetime shorter than the whole operation, so if it expires between the mutation and the restore, the actor cannot authenticate to roll back and leaves the resource in the mutated -- usually worse -- state, with no recovery path held in-session)." as NonEmptyStr,

    "INVALID STATE: an actuation whose rollback is gated on a credential that can expire mid-run, where the mutation is already applied and no pre-fetched recovery authorization or idempotent self-restore exists. The precondition for undoing the effect is acquired AFTER the effect, from an authority that can refuse by then." as NonEmptyStr,

    "WHY IT SURVIVES REVIEW/TESTING: the happy path -- mutate, observe, restore -- works for the entire window the credential is valid, which covers every quick run. The failure only appears when the operation runs long enough for the credential to lapse, and short-lived tokens (≈1h) make that a function of how many experiment iterations happen, not of the code. So it passes in the small and bites in the long session that actually needed it." as NonEmptyStr,

    "SPECIMEN (2026-09-17, srv5 fabric leg qsfp56-dd-1-1). A session forced both link ends to 100G to rule out a cause, fetching the Spark administrator password and the switch credential over GCP Secret Manager REST with a pasted `gcloud` access token. The token expired mid-experiment; the restore step's credential re-fetch returned 401, so neither the host nor the switch could be put back, and srv5 was left forced-100G / NO-CARRIER (down) until the operator supplied a fresh token. Recovery then also needed an out-of-band `ip link down/up` on the host, found by trial, because re-enabling autoneg alone did not retrain the link. Recorded as gap G16 in docs/plans/fabric-switch-100g-convergence-gap-analysis.md and in the actuation log." as NonEmptyStr,

    "THE DISCRIMINATOR THAT BOUNDS IT: it bites OUT-OF-BAND actuation -- a session driving a live resource with per-step credential fetches -- where the mutation is not idempotently self-restoring. It does NOT bite the modeled actuation path (e.g. gunbc.spark.managed_grant_install run through fleet-converge), where the credential is materialized ONCE per run into a run-local file, removed in the same step, and the whole mutate-observe-verify unit is a single bounded run whose lifetime the credential covers by construction. The tell is the credential re-fetch on the restore leg, not on the forward leg." as NonEmptyStr,

    "RUNG: found at MITIGATABLE, and below the ordinary floor for actuation discipline -- the effect is applied and the recovery is not held, so an expiry leaves the subject unrestored with only a human able to recover it. It is a property of an actuation SCRIPT, not of an Accepted .dag program, so the ladder applies to the DISCIPLINE (does an actuation hold its own rollback authorization before it mutates) rather than to a modeled state." as NonEmptyStr,

    "CEILING: a mutating actuator that holds ALL authorization for both the forward action AND the recovery before the first effect, where no rollback depends on post-effect credential acquisition, and every unsuccessful terminal (refusal, timeout, interruption, Polling, producer disagreement) includes VERIFIED restoration of the captured pre-state read back from the subject. A bounded single run is NOT sufficient on its own -- it does not by itself guarantee the credential outlives the mutation, that the process survives to roll back, or that a partial mutation is restored -- so the ceiling is the held-recovery-authorization + verified-rollback invariant, not merely making the modeled path the only path. Hardening the session path (pre-fetch both credentials and hold them across the whole experiment) is the interim mitigation and is strictly weaker than an actuator that structurally cannot begin a mutation without already holding its rollback authority." as NonEmptyStr,

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a two-ended converge actuator lands that (1) obtains and validates all forward and recovery authorization before the first effect, (2) captures host and switch pre-state, (3) applies the candidate to both endpoints, (4) accepts only bilateral agreement on the required link state, (5) on every other terminal restores both captured pre-states WITHOUT fetching new authorization, (6) reads both endpoints back and makes verified restoration part of the terminal, and (7) removes credentials only after confirmed success or confirmed rollback; and the out-of-band session-curl/ssh path is retired. SUFFICIENT FOR: no live link mode is mutated by a path that can terminate with the endpoints intentionally divergent or whose rollback re-fetches a credential mid-operation." as NonEmptyStr,

    "REVIEW TELL: an actuation from a session or script that fetches a short-lived credential per step, mutates a live resource, and whose RESTORE step re-fetches that same credential. Ask: if the credential expires after the mutation and before the restore, what state is the resource left in, and is there a recovery authorization already held that does not depend on re-fetching?" as NonEmptyStr,

    "CONSUMPTION: this per-class record joins the derived gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster recurring_failure_mode_roster. It records the class; it neither repairs the out-of-band path nor claims the modeled path is built." as NonEmptyStr,
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
